WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.290
- [Instructor] We're
told express the point

00:00:02.290 --> 00:00:06.310
on the number line as both
a fraction and a decimal.

00:00:06.310 --> 00:00:08.923
So pause this video and have a go at that.

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:11.420
All right, now let's do this together.

00:00:11.420 --> 00:00:14.650
So we can see that the point in question,

00:00:14.650 --> 00:00:18.300
it's at a higher value than
four and it's less than five.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:20.410
So greater than four, less than five.

00:00:20.410 --> 00:00:22.900
And the space between four and five

00:00:22.900 --> 00:00:26.730
is divided into one,
two, three, four, five,

00:00:26.730 --> 00:00:30.740
six, seven, eight,
nine, 10 equal sections.

00:00:30.740 --> 00:00:34.770
So each of these hash marks
represent an extra tenth.

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:38.350
So this is four, then this
is four and one tenth,

00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:42.040
and now this is four right
over here and two tenths.

00:00:42.040 --> 00:00:43.840
So we could write this,
if we wanted to write it

00:00:43.840 --> 00:00:45.360
as a fraction or as a mixed number,

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:48.010
this would be four and two tenths.

00:00:48.010 --> 00:00:50.090
And if we wanted to
write that as a decimal,

00:00:50.090 --> 00:00:53.590
that would be four, and
then in the tenths place,

00:00:53.590 --> 00:00:55.690
well we have two tenths.

00:00:55.690 --> 00:00:57.220
And that's it, we're done.

00:00:57.220 --> 00:00:58.473
Let's do another example.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:02.870
So here, we're once again asked

00:01:02.870 --> 00:01:04.580
to express the point on the number line

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:06.400
as both a fraction and a decimal,

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:07.840
but this one's a little bit different.

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:09.230
Let's see how you can, if you can identify

00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:11.450
how it is different and
answer the question.

00:01:11.450 --> 00:01:14.050
So pause this video, and
once again have a go at it.

00:01:14.930 --> 00:01:18.440
All right, so here, our point,

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:21.260
it's not between two whole numbers.

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:24.420
It's actually between two tenths.

00:01:24.420 --> 00:01:26.400
We're between three and two tenths

00:01:26.400 --> 00:01:28.780
and three and three tenths.

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:30.810
So this is between three and two tenths

00:01:30.810 --> 00:01:32.170
and three and three tenths.

00:01:32.170 --> 00:01:36.290
So each of these hash marks,
which are a tenth of a tenth.

00:01:36.290 --> 00:01:39.360
So they would actually be a hundredth.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:42.490
So one way to think about
it, you could view 3.2

00:01:42.490 --> 00:01:46.980
or three and two tenths as
three and 20 hundredths.

00:01:46.980 --> 00:01:48.820
And you could view three and three tenths

00:01:48.820 --> 00:01:51.540
as three and 30 hundredths.

00:01:51.540 --> 00:01:54.775
And so this is three and 20 hundredths.

00:01:54.775 --> 00:01:59.450
This is three and 21 hundredths,
three and 22 hundredths.

00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:04.450
So this point right over here
is three and 22 hundredths,

00:02:05.600 --> 00:02:08.130
22 hundredths, and of
course, you could also

00:02:08.130 --> 00:02:09.650
write that as a mixed number.

00:02:09.650 --> 00:02:14.650
That is three and 22 over 100.

00:02:16.590 --> 00:02:19.030
Now another way that you
could've approached it is

00:02:19.030 --> 00:02:24.030
hey, I'm starting at 3.2 or
three and two tenths, and

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:25.873
so I'm starting here at 3.2.

00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:30.580
And then I'm going to add to
that, not just one hundredth,

00:02:30.580 --> 00:02:34.640
but two hundredths, so it would be three,

00:02:34.640 --> 00:02:38.350
two tenths, and then two hundredths.

00:02:38.350 --> 00:02:39.910
And there you have it, we've expressed it

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:41.953
as both a fraction and a decimal.

